\paragraph{Monotonicity.}
The two diagnostics exhibit opposing monotonicity in $b$:

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop:monotone}
$\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}(b)$ is non-decreasing in $b$, and strictly increasing whenever the batch contains at least one sample with $q_i \in (0,1)$ and $r_i$ is not constant.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By construction, $w_i = (1{-}q_i)^b$ assigns higher weight to lower $q_i$.
As $b$ increases, weights concentrate on low-$q$ samples.
Formally, $\frac{d\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}}{db} = \mathrm{Cov}_{\pi}\!\bigl(r_i,\, \ln(1{-}q_i)\bigr) \geq 0$, where positivity follows from the monotone relationship between the label proxy $r_i$ and log-weight $\ln(1{-}q_i)$.
Detector quality determines how tight this coupling is; we validate the proxy empirically in Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_validation}.
Full derivation in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
\end{proof}

\noindent $\mathrm{ESS}(b)$ is monotonically decreasing in $b$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:ess_mono}, Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}): larger exponents concentrate the weight distribution, a standard property of exponential tilting~\citep{kish1965survey,owen2013mc}.

